  "notes": "## Intuition\n\nA simple fair method draws a random value from the ones not yet used, removes it, and repeats; removing from the middle of an array makes that O(n²). The **Fisher–Yates shuffle** does the same draws in place: walk from the last position down, and swap each position `i` with a random position `j` in `0..i`. The part after `i` is final, and the part up to `i` is the pool still to draw from, so every order arises from exactly one sequence of draws and all `n!` orders are equally likely.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. In the constructor, store a copy of `nums` as `original`, so nothing a caller does later can change it.\n2. `reset()` returns a fresh copy of `original`.\n3. `shuffle()` copies `original` into `shuffled`.\n4. For `i` from the last index down to 1, pick `j` uniformly in `0..i` and swap `shuffled[i]` with `shuffled[j]`.\n5. Return `shuffled`.\n\n## Example 1, step by step\n\n`original = [1,2,3]`. In the first `shuffle()`, say `i = 2` draws `j = 1`: swapping gives `[1,3,2]`. Then `i = 1` draws `j = 0`: swapping gives `[3,1,2]`, which is returned. `reset()` returns `[1,2,3]`. The second `shuffle()` starts again from `[1,2,3]`; with draws `j = 1` and then `j = 1` it returns `[1,3,2]`. Other draws give other orders, and all six are equally likely.\n\n## Complexity\n\n- Time: O(n) per `shuffle()` and per `reset()` (copying the array).\n- Space: O(n) for the stored copy and the returned array.\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n- Draw `j` from `0..i` inclusive. Using `0..i-1` (Sattolo's algorithm) never leaves a value in place, so some orders can never appear.\n- Swapping each position with a random index from the whole array makes `n^n` equally likely runs, which cannot split evenly over `n!` orders: the result is biased.\n- Keep `original` untouched. Shuffling it in place makes `reset()` return a shuffled array.\n\n## Variables\n\n| Name | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `original` | the untouched copy of `nums` |\n| `shuffled` | the copy being shuffled and returned |\n| `i` | the position being finalised, moving from the end to the front |\n| `j` | the random position in `0..i` whose value moves to `i` |",
  "narration": {
    "3": "Keep a private copy of nums so later changes cannot touch it.",
